From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Fri, 21 Jan 94 11:35:50 GMT
Subject: [94-1-362] The Go-Go Future

>Mark Plus.
>
>developing a faster
>computer chip won't revolutionize current business uses of
>computers.

No, but it will enable revolutionary new business uses of computers.
Like desktop publishing.

>Sales of 486-based PC's didn't take off until they
>came down in price to be competitive with 386-based PC's, which
>is the market's way of saying that the additional speed did not
>add that much value.

Or that the few who did see higher value in the additional speed financed
the price reduction for those who weren't as imaginative.

>Most businesses are
>not being significantly hampered by lack of computing power.  

Most businesses are hampered by a failure to utilize what computing power
there already is available to them, such as the restaurant menu computerization
example I already gave, or the way many data-entry procedures have been
"virtualized" from hardcopy, rather than truly computerized, such as the
case I heard about the other day in which, rather than integrate the data
needed on several forms into one entry, it was done just as it was on paper:
name, address, etc. on this page, the next page, etc.

The corollary to things like the 486 dropping to the price of 386s is that the
price of 386s drops, as well, making that amount of computing power more
affordable to those who haven't utilized it yet.  Another example is the new
laserprinter we just got for about $1000, which has higher resolution and
speed than the one we got about 5 years ago for much more money.  State-of-
the art computing power will probably stay at about the same price because
that's what the market will bear, but as it advances it makes the previous
"states-of-the-art" cheaper, more affordable, and more able to augment the
productivity of more parts of the labor market.

>The real test for the "So-So" thesis,
>however, will come when we look at Japan's economic situation in
>about ten years.

Wouldn't a better test be interest rate trends?  If you're right, then
the general trend in interest rates should be a decline, yet I don't
think that has been the case.

