From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 94 14:33:11 WET
Subject: [94-1-375] META: Bug fixes and new feature

  Firstly, I just killed a few bugs, most notably, the bug involving
::resend thread. Some of you may have noticed that if you do two
::resend thread's in a row, only one of them gets processed. I've known
about this bug for months but couldn't find it. It turns out, I commited
what's called "variable suicide". The list software is written in perl
and uses dynamic scoping rather than lexical scoping like C. As a result,
your variables can get clobbered by a complex series of indirections
which are really hard to keep track if you're not paying attention
and used to programming with lexical scoping. Anyway, I feel like
shooting the inventor of dynamic scoping.

  Secondly, resend has a new feature -- ranges. Instead of giving
resend a single message number as an argument when retrieving articles
by number, you can give a range, or a comma separated list of ranges or
individual message numbers.

Example:

::resend #10
   -- resends message #10 (single message sent)
::resend #20-#30
   -- resends messages #20 through #30 (in digest format)
::resend #10,#20-#30
   -- resends message #10, and numbers #20 through #30 (in one digest)
::resend #1,#2,#3
   -- resends messages #1,#2 and #3 in a digest

NOTE: DO NOT MAKE THE MISTAKE OF LEAVING OFF '#' ON THE RANGES
    
  For instance, if you write ::resend #20-30
                                          ^  missing '#'
  the list software will not interpret it right. Especially when you
give fully qualified numbers such as #94-1-20-30
                                             ^ missing '#'

 You can see why. The '-' character is overloaded operating as the
message number separator and the range separator. (In retrospect,
perhaps I should have used '/' as the separated between year/month/msgnum.
Oh well.)


-Ray

