by the Scholastics; or (c) some product of the two?  I will be exploring this
question as applied to Objectivism in my paper on Pancritical Rationalism
for Extro 1.  My take on this is that dogmatization is generally (c) -- 
a combination of the structure of a system of ideas and the psychology 
of people who use them.  Some systems of ideas are more vulnerable than others
to dogmatizing but, with enough need for dogma on the part of persons 
aware of the system, almost *any* intellectual system can be dogmatized.  
I think we see that in the workings of the state-science system today, 
where established views use the mantle of science to crush alternative 
approaches.  (I'm not suggesting that "anything goes" in science,
just that science can be misused to protect entrenched interests.)

Christianity and Marxism provide clear examples of systems that are especially
conducive to faith and dogmatization.  (Any religion -- defined as "a system of
*faith* and worship" -- will be highly conducive to this.)  Marxism embodies 
certain ideas that guarantee irrationality and dogma in most Marxists:  For
example, the concept of "false consciousness" asserts that anyone who argues 
against Marxism is suffering from ideological mystification resulting from
an intellectual superstructure automatically and necessarily generated by the
underlying relations of production.  (Marx never explained how it was that he 
and Engels -- an industrialist and so objectively part of the oppressive
bourgeoisie -- managed to step outside these powerful forces.]  Similarly, 
Marxists are find of saying that "you're either part of the solution 
or part of the problem", i.e., you must stand fully with them, otherwise 
you're the *enemy*.  [By contrast I advocate alliances with other groups, 
transhumanist or not, on issues where we share values and goals.]

Is Extropianism, as expressed in the Principles and in Extropy articles, 
especially conducive to dogmatization?  In my initial response to Mr. May I 
noted various ways in which I have strived to shield Extropianism from this 
danger.  [It was May's complete obliviousness to my efforts that riled me.]
I worry about the concept of the Singularity (and will eventually address 
those worries in an article) and prefer the view, shared by Mr. May with 
some others (Nick Szabo for one, I believe), that "singularity" is an 
inappropriate metaphor, since the changes will take place at differing speeds
in different areas of technology and culture (and, I would add, human 
factors will slow down technology-driven acceleration).  "Singularity" is 
uncomfortably close to "Rapture".  I have also supported the pursuit of a 
gradual integration of ourselves with our technology (transbiomorphosis) 
over the idea of a sudden, discontinuous uploading.  Not that I dispute 
the possibility of uploading, just that I suspect a gradual process
to be more probable, and its prospect to be less likely to generate 
expectations of an idyllic post-upload life (as in uploading = heaven).  
Fortunately, the description of life as uploaded persons presented by the most
well known upload enthusiast (Hans Moravec) sound nothing like the lazy, 
easy life of the Christian heaven.

Any system of ideas, any codification of values, is at some risk of lending 
itself to the common need for faith, for certainty, and for easy answers.  
Extropianism is no exception.  I don't think it's been much of a problem 
*so far* because its early adopters have primarily been persons of high 
intelligence, critical thinking, and low tolerance for bullshit 
and mindlessness.  If our movement continues to 
grow at its current rates, we could see many more people calling themselves
"Extropians" who are not so independent-minded.  I worry about this.  I 
thoroughly encourage ideas for preventive measures.  This concern is the major
reason why I will be presenting a paper to Extro 1 on Pancritical Rationalism;
this epistemology, among its other virtues, appears to constitute a powerful
inoculation against a hardening of the orthodoxies.  The challenge is to find
ways of building this epistemology (and attitude) into Extropian institutions
and practices.

I am all in favor of a session at Extro 1 to generate means of warding off
encrustations of dogma.  If this area seems important to you, and you have some
ideas, please let me know if you would like to sit on a panel at the 
conference.

>Sure, rituals are all around us. I just don't like the trappings of
>Extropianism that are developing. The "Extropian music" threads that
>appear every few months, the "Extropian diet" debates, and now the
>"Extropian Relationships" business. Sounds like the cult of
>Objectivism, or Scientific Socialism. 

I'm not going to write out detailed thoughts on each of these topic areas,just
a couple of comments.  Talk of "Extropian music", "Extropian diet", etc., are 
(or should be, in my view) shorthand for "music likely to be enjoyed by 
extropically-minded folks, or music that invigorates and energizes" 
and "diets that contribute towards values shared by those who think 
of themselves as Extropian" -- i.e., health, longevity, enjoyment.  
This does not mean (as in Rand's writing) that certain musicians 
must be liked by all Extropians (responses to music are personaland 
determined by many hard to define factors; I like techno music for its energy
and high-tech sound, other highly extropian people don't get much out of it).
Some music could plausibly be called non-extropian (small "e") or even 
anti-extropian, such as music that glorifies mindless destruction, despair,
hatred, or which attacks civilization, freedom, and advancement.  So, there is
no mandatory "Extropian music", either in general style nor in particular
artists.  We can only draw very general and fuzzy lines around what we want to
regard as extropian music.  Similarly for diets (where we must also recognize 
that the tradeoff between health and pleasure is one to be decided by each 
individual, and will be affected by many disputable estimations -- such as 
degree of confidence in the progress of life extension research, or cryonics 
research, personal interest in looking physically attractive, weight given 
to gustatory pleasure, and so on.)

Upward and Outward!

